    "body": "### Problem\n\nIf I try to edit a file on a remote IP address via `scp://`, Neovim fails to open it. I am not even prompted to enter the SSH password, and instead I just get the following error message:\n\n```\nscp: Connection closed\n\nshell signalled an error                                                                                                                                                                                          \nPress ENTER or type command to continue\n```\n\nIf, instead, I try to open a directory, all files have a \"Permission denied\" in their place, which is consistent with the fact that I never got prompted to input a password.\n\nNotice that this only happens if the machine is addressed by IP address. If addressed by name, it works.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n- Have an SSH-enabled remote machine that you can access\n\n- Run a command of this form to edit a file in it: `nvim scp://[username]@[IP address]//path/to/file` (alternatively, `/path/to/directory/` to open a directory)\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe remote file or directory is opened in Neovim.\n\nThis is the behavior in Vim, as can be verified by running the same commands with `vim` instead of `nvim`.\n\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nv0.13.0-dev-1681+ga73243faa5 (same on stable)\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nNo\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nArch Linux x86_64\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nKonsole 26.08.1\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nNightly AppImage (same on stable from official Arch repo)",
